Two kinds of file live here, and the lantern plays both out of the same
rotation:

    *.wav   8-bit unsigned mono recordings, streamed off flash by audio.py
    *.mid   scores, synthesised as they play by midi.py

Copy them to the board:

    mpremote mkdir :tracks
    mpremote cp hardware/pumpkin-house/tracks/*.wav :tracks/
    mpremote cp hardware/pumpkin-house/tracks/*.mid :tracks/

A .wav is the arrangement you actually rendered and costs about 20 KB a
second; a .mid is a few KB for the whole piece but comes out as a six-voice
square-wave chiptune, because synthesising it is what the board can do in
the time between flame ticks. Roughly: recordings for atmosphere - thunder,
wind, a door - and scores for anything with a tune in it.

The folder is called `tracks` and NOT `audio` on purpose. MicroPython's
import namespace is flat and a directory beats a module of the same name:
with a `/audio` folder in the board's root, `import audio` silently returns
an empty namespace package instead of `audio.py`, `audio.Speaker` stops
existing, and the lantern runs with no speaker and no sound at all. It
degrades quietly because `main.build_speaker()` catches it, which is right
for a missing amplifier and very confusing for this. Do not rename it back.

`dirge.mid` is here as the one committed example - an original eight-bar
waltz in D minor, 557 bytes for 15.6 seconds. The same music as a .wav at
11 kHz would be about 170 KB, which is the whole argument for the format on
a board with 1.3 MB of flash.

Filename order is the playing order, so prefix them 10-, 20-, 30-.
The ffmpeg encoding recipe and the flash budget are in the help app,
under Hardware -> /hardware/pumpkin-house -> "Audio files".
